    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                The velocity <m>v(t)</m> of a skydiver falling to the ground is
                governed by
                <md>
                    m\frac{dv}{dt} = mg - kv^2,
                </md>
                where <m>m</m> is the mass of the skydiver, <m>g</m> is the
                acceleration due to gravity, and <m>{k \gt 0}</m> is a constant
                related to the amount of air resistance.
                Note that we can rewrite this equation as
                <md>
                    \frac{dv}{dt} = -\frac{k}{m}\left(v^2 - \frac{mg}{k}\right).
                </md>
                Assume <m>v(0) = 0</m> and that the downwards direction is
                positive.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the positive fixed point of the differential equation.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The terminal velocity is also called the limiting velocity,
                    as it is the limit of the velocity in free fall as time goes
                    to infinity.
                    Take <m>m=180</m> kg, <m>g = 32</m> ft/<m>s^2</m>, and
                    <m>k = 0.2</m> kg/ft.
                    Find the numerical solution to the ODE.
                    Plot the numerical solution, along with the line
                    <m>y = v^*</m>, where <m>v^*</m> is the fixed point you
                    bound in Part (a).
                    How does the terminal velocity compare to the fixed point?
                    What does this mean?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>